C("saradwat","Saradwat","Gautama|son of Gotama","shuh-RUD-vut","male","sage (archer)","Rishi",
  "Archer-sage whose seed, spilled at the sight of an Apsara, produces Kripa and Kripi",
  "Born with arrows, son of Gautama, he excels in the science of arms; Indra sends the Apsara Janapadi to distract him, and his seed falls on a clump of reeds, producing twins (§130). Santanu adopts them; Saradwat later teaches Kripa the four branches of arms (§130).",
  "austere|martial","",f"An austere Indian archer-sage, lean firm voice, {A}","130","non_speaking")
C("kripa","Kripa","Gautama|son of Saradwat|Kripacharya","KRI-puh","male","human (Brahmana preceptor)","adult",
  "Twin born of Saradwat's seed, raised by Santanu; first preceptor in arms to the Kuru princes",
  "Found with his sister in a clump of reeds and raised by Santanu out of compassion (kripa) (§130). He becomes the princes' first teacher of arms (§129–§130). Drona lives privately in his house on arrival (§133). At the tournament he demands Karna's lineage (§138).",
  "fair-minded|steady|traditional","steady|stern formality",f"An Indian preceptor, plain steady baritone, fair-minded, {A}","129 130 133 138","non_speaking")
C("kripi","Kripi","","KRI-pee","female","human","young woman",
  "Kripa's twin sister; wife of Drona; mother of Aswatthaman",
  "Born with Kripa from Saradwat's seed and raised by Santanu (§130). Drona marries her, and she bears Aswatthaman (§131, §133).",
  "devoted","",f"A modest Indian woman, soft voice, {A}","130 131 133","non_speaking")
C("bharadwaja","Bharadwaja","","bhuh-RUD-vaa-juh","male","sage","great Rishi",
  "Great Rishi; father of Drona; friend of king Prishata",
  "At the sight of the Apsara Ghritachi his seed falls into a vessel (drona), from which Drona is born (§131, §168). A friend of king Prishata, his hermitage is where Drona and Drupada study together (§131).",
  "austere","",f"A grave old Indian sage, deep voice, {A}","131 168","non_speaking")
C("drona","Drona","son of Bharadwaja|Bharadwaja's son","DRO-nuh","male","human (Brahmana weapons-master)","adult",
  "Son of Bharadwaja; preceptor of the Kurus and Pandavas; Drupada's estranged friend",
  "Born in a vessel from Bharadwaja's seed (§131). He studies with Drupada, then obtains Rama's (Parasurama's) weapons (§131, §168). Insulted by Drupada as a poor man claiming friendship (§132), he comes to Hastinapura, retrieves the princes' ball from a well, and is appointed their teacher by Bhishma (§133). He favours Arjuna (§134), demands Ekalavya's thumb (§134), tests the princes at the bird's eye (§134), and gives Arjuna the Brahmasira (§135). As his fee he has the princes capture Drupada, and takes half the Panchala kingdom (§140). He counsels returning the Pandavas their share (§207).",
  "proud|exacting|partial to Arjuna|wounded by poverty","hard precision|pride|bitter grievance|affection for Arjuna",
  f"A formidable Indian Brahmin weapons-master, hard precise baritone, proud, {A}","131 132 133 134 135 140 168 207")
C("aswatthaman","Aswatthaman","","ush-VUT-thaa-maa","male","human (Brahmana warrior)","youth",
  "Son of Drona and Kripi; neighs like Uchchaihsravas at birth",
  "At birth he neighs like the celestial horse Uchchaihsravas, so a voice names him Aswatthaman (§131). Drona's poverty, when the boy cannot get milk, drives Drona to seek Drupada (§131–§133). He trains with the princes (§134), and Duryodhana counts on him (§144).",
  "spirited|ambitious","eagerness",f"A young Indian warrior, bright sharp tenor, {A}","131 133 134 144","non_speaking")
C("drupada","Drupada","Yajnasena|son of Prishata|king of Panchala","DROO-puh-duh","male","human (Panchala king)","boy→mature king",
  "King of Panchala; Drona's estranged friend; father of Dhrishtadyumna and Draupadi",
  "Son of Prishata, he studies with Drona at Bharadwaja's hermitage (§131). As king he spurns Drona's claim of friendship (§132). Arjuna captures him and Drona takes half his kingdom (§140). Seeking revenge, he performs a sacrifice through Yaja and Upayaja, from which Dhrishtadyumna and Draupadi are born (§168–§169). He holds Draupadi's swayamvara with a hard bow and an aerial target (§186–§187). He is troubled by the five-husband marriage until Vyasa explains it (§195–§199).",
  "proud|vengeful|dignified|devout","haughty disdain|humiliation|vengeful resolve|paternal anxiety",
  f"A mature Indian king, rich proud baritone, dignified with simmering grievance, {A}","131 132 140 168 169 187 195 197 199")
C("ekalavya","Ekalavya","son of Hiranyadhanus","ay-kuh-LUV-yuh","male","human (Nishada prince)","youth",
  "Nishada prince who learns archery before a clay image of Drona and gives up his thumb",
  "Son of Hiranyadhanus, king of the Nishadas; Drona refuses him as a pupil (§134). He practises before a clay image of Drona and silences the Pandavas' dog with seven arrows (§134). When Drona asks for his right thumb as a teacher's fee, he cheerfully cuts it off (§134).",
  "devoted|humble|determined","earnest devotion|cheerful sacrifice",f"A young Indian tribal prince, earnest humble tenor, devoted, {A}","134","single_section_speaker")
C("kanika","Kanika","","KUH-ni-kuh","male","human (Brahmana minister)","elderly counsellor",
  "Dhritarashtra's minister who teaches crooked statecraft",
  "Asked by Dhritarashtra how to deal with powerful rivals, he gives a long discourse on ruthless policy, including the fable of the jackal, and urges him to destroy the Pandavas by craft (§142). Dhritarashtra recalls his advice (§144), and he is named again later (§203).",
  "cunning|cold|eloquent","cold calculation|insinuation",f"An elderly Indian minister, smooth cold deliberate voice, {A}","142 144 203")
C("purochana","Purochana","","poo-RO-chuh-nuh","male","human (Duryodhana's agent)","adult",
  "Duryodhana's minister who builds the house of lac at Varanavata",
  "Duryodhana commissions him to build an inflammable house at Varanavata and burn the Pandavas (§143, §146). He welcomes them and lodges them there (§148). Bhima sets the house on fire while Purochana sleeps in it, and he dies (§150).",
  "treacherous|obsequious","ingratiating|false warmth",f"An Indian courtier, ingratiating oily tenor, treacherous, {A}","143 146 148 150","non_speaking")
