C("chitraratha","Chitraratha","Angaraparna|the Gandharva|Gandharva","CHIT-ruh-ruh-thuh (ung-guh-ruh-PUR-nuh)","male","celestial (Gandharva king)","ageless",
  "Gandharva king defeated by Arjuna on the Ganga; tells the tales of Tapati, Vasishtha and Aurva",
  "Bathing with his women in the Ganga at night, he challenges the Pandavas. Arjuna burns his chariot with the Agneya weapon, and his wife Kumbhinasi begs for his life (§172). Renamed Chitraratha, he gives the Pandavas the knowledge of Chakshushi and horses (§172). He calls Arjuna 'Tapatya' and narrates Samvarana and Tapati (§173–§175), Vasishtha and Viswamitra (§175, §177–§178), Kalmashapada (§178–§179, §184), Parasara (§180, §183) and, through Vasishtha's narration, Aurva (§182), and he advises them to take Dhaumya as priest (§183, §185).",
  "boastful|then gracious|erudite storyteller","arrogance|humbled gratitude|storytelling relish",
  f"A celestial Gandharva king, musical baritone, boastful at first, then a rich courtly storyteller, {A}","172 173 175 177 178 183 185")
