C("vasishtha","Vasishtha","Apava|son of Varuna","vuh-SHISH-thuh","male","sage","ancient",
  "Great Rishi; owner of Nandini; curses the Vasus; Viswamitra's rival; grandfather of Parasara",
  "As Apava he curses the Vasus for stealing Nandini (§99). He wins Tapati for Samvarana (§175). Viswamitra tries to take Nandini by force, and Nandini's warriors defeat his army; Vasishtha forbears (§177). Kalmashapada, possessed through Viswamitra, devours his hundred sons including Saktri; in grief he tries to die, but the rivers and fire refuse him (§178–§179). He hears the unborn Parasara reciting the Vedas, returns to life (§179–§180), restrains Parasara's wrath with the tale of Aurva (§180–§182), and frees Kalmashapada from the curse (§179, §184).",
  "forbearing|serene|grief-stricken|wise","profound calm|anguish|tender hope|gentle admonition",
  f"An ancient Indian sage, profound calm bass, forbearing and luminous, {A}","99 175 177 178 179 180 181 182 184")
C("nandini","Nandini","the cow of plenty|Naridini (misprint)","NUN-di-nee","female","animal (divine cow)","ageless",
  "Vasishtha's wish-fulfilling cow, daughter of Surabhi",
  "Daughter of Surabhi by Kasyapa, she gives Vasishtha all he desires (§99). The Vasus steal her (§99). Beaten by Viswamitra's men, she asks Vasishtha whether he abandons her, and creates hordes of Pahlavas, Sakas and other warriors from her body (§177).",
  "gentle|wrathful when abused","plaintive|fierce",f"A gentle low feminine voice that rises into a fierce roar, {A}","99 177")
C("saktri","Saktri","Sakti","SHUK-tree","male","sage","adult",
  "Eldest son of Vasishtha; curses Kalmashapada to become a Rakshasa and is devoured by him",
  "Refuses to give way on a narrow path to king Kalmashapada, who strikes him with a whip; Saktri curses him to become a man-eater (§178). Possessed by the Rakshasa Kinkara, the king devours Saktri first (§178). Father of Parasara by Adrisyanti (§179–§180).",
  "proud|unyielding","indignation",f"A proud Indian sage, sharp indignant baritone, {A}","178 179 180","non_speaking")
C("adrisyanti","Adrisyanti","wife of Saktri","uh-drish-YUN-tee","female","human (Rishi's wife)","young widow",
  "Saktri's widow; mother of Parasara",
  "Follows the grieving Vasishtha; the Vedic chant he hears comes from the unborn child in her womb (§179). She protects Vasishtha from the Rakshasa-king and gives birth to Parasara (§179–§180).",
  "gentle|steadfast","grief|hope",f"A young Indian widow, gentle steadfast voice, {A}","179 180")
C("parasara","Parasara","son of Saktri","PUH-raa-shuh-ruh","male","sage","boy→Rishi",
  "Son of Saktri and Adrisyanti; begins a sacrifice to destroy the Rakshasas; father of Vyasa",
  "Born after his father's death and named because he restored Vasishtha's will to live (§180). Learning his father's fate, he resolves to destroy the worlds; Vasishtha dissuades him with Aurva's story (§180–§182). He performs a Rakshasa-destroying sacrifice and stops at Pulastya's plea (§183). By the maiden Satyavati he fathers Vyasa (§63, §105).",
  "intense|wrathful|ultimately restrained","grief|wrath|restraint",f"An Indian sage, smooth intense baritone, {A}","63 105 180 182 183","non_speaking")
C("kalmashapada","Kalmashapada","Saudasa","kul-maa-shuh-PAA-duh","male","human (Ikshvaku king; possessed)","adult",
  "Ikshvaku king cursed by Saktri to become a man-eater; devours Vasishtha's sons",
  "Strikes Saktri on a narrow path and is cursed (§178). Viswamitra has the Rakshasa Kinkara enter him, and he devours Saktri and Vasishtha's other sons (§178). A Brahmani whose husband he devoured curses him (§184). Vasishtha later frees him, and at his request Vasishtha begets a son, Asmaka, on queen Madayanti (§179, §122, §184).",
  "tormented|savage under curse|penitent","savage hunger|torment|remorse",f"A cursed Indian king, ragged harsh baritone, tormented, {A}","122 178 179 184","non_speaking")
C("aurva","Aurva","","OUR-vuh","male","sage","Rishi",
  "Bhrigu-line Rishi born from his mother's thigh, whose wrath threatens the worlds",
  "The Kshatriyas slaughter the Bhrigus, and he bursts from his mother's thigh, blinding the pursuers (§181). His wrath, ready to destroy the worlds, is checked by the Pitris and cast into the sea as the horse-headed fire (§181–§182).",
  "wrathful|righteous","volcanic wrath|reluctant restraint",f"A young Indian sage, blazing intense voice, {A}","181 182")
