C("krishna","Krishna","Vasudeva|Kesava|Janardana|Govinda|Madhava|Achyuta|Hrishikesa|Damodara","KRISH-nuh","male","human (Vrishni prince; Narayana incarnate)","young man",
  "Vrishni prince, Narayana incarnate; recognises the Pandavas at the swayamvara; Arjuna's friend; helps burn Khandava",
  "At Draupadi's swayamvara he recognises the disguised Pandavas and restrains the kings (§189, §191–§192). He visits them with Balarama (§193) and sends wedding gifts (§201). He goes with Vidura to Hastinapura and to Khandavaprastha (§209). He welcomes Arjuna at Prabhasa (§220), advises him to carry off his sister Subhadra (§221), and calms the angry Vrishnis (§223). With Arjuna he helps Agni burn Khandava, receiving a discus (§224–§230), and he spares Maya (§230).",
  "charismatic|wise|playful|decisive","warm amusement|calm authority|affection",
  f"A charismatic young Indian prince, mellifluous warm baritone, playful and wise, {A}","189 191 193 209 220 221 223 224 227 230")
C("balarama","Balarama","Valadeva|Baladeva|Halayudha|Sankarshana|Rama (Krishna's brother)","BUH-luh-raa-muh","male","human (Vrishni prince)","young man",
  "Elder brother of Krishna; angry at Subhadra's abduction until pacified",
  "He attends Draupadi's swayamvara and rejoices on hearing of the Pandavas' escape (§189, §191). He visits the Pandavas with Krishna (§193). Furious at Arjuna's abduction of Subhadra, he rouses the Vrishnis, until Krishna persuades him (§222–§223).",
  "hot-tempered|hearty|generous","booming wrath|hearty joy",f"A powerful Indian prince, loud hearty baritone, quick-tempered, {A}","191 193 222 223","non_speaking")
C("parasurama","Rama (son of Jamadagni)","Rama|Parasurama|Jamadagnya|son of Jamadagni|Rama of Bhrigu's race","PUH-ruh-shoo-raa-muh","male","human (Bhargava warrior-sage)","ancient",
  "Bhargava warrior-sage who exterminated the Kshatriyas; gives Drona all his weapons",
  "After twenty-one times ridding the earth of Kshatriyas, he retires to Mahendra (§64). Before leaving for the forest he gives Drona all his weapons and their mysteries (§131, §168). He is cited as the standard of martial skill (§55, §100).",
  "terrible|generous|world-weary","renunciation|stern grandeur",f"An ancient Indian warrior-sage, stern resonant voice, {A}","64 131 168")
